{"bill":{"id":"119-s-2618","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"2618","title":"MORE USDA Grants Act","introducedDate":"2025-07-31","latestActionDate":"2025-07-31","latestActionText":"Read twice and referred to the Committee on Agriculture, Nutrition, and Forestry.","policyArea":"Agriculture and Food","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001113","cosponsorBioguideIds":["D000618","S001150","C000880","R000584"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"More Opportunities for Rural Economies from USDA Grants Act or the MORE USDA Grants Act This bill expands access to qualifying Department of Agriculture (USDA) grant programs for local governments or tribal governments located within a high-density public land county. Under the bill, these are counties (1) that have a population of not more than 100,000 people, and (2) where the federal government owns or manages more than 50% of the land. Qualifying USDA grant programs include the Rural Business Development Grants, the Community Facilities grant program, the Distance Learning and Telemedicine Grants program, and other specified grant programs administered by USDA rural development agencies or by USDA. Under the bill, any requirement for local matching funds under a qualifying grant program must be reduced by 50% for a local government or tribal government within a high-density public land county. On request, USDA must provide additional technical assistance to these local governments or tribal governments before and during the annual application period for each qualifying grant program. Further, USDA must prioritize grant applications from these local governments and tribal governments that have not received support in the previous 10 years under the qualifying grant program. For qualifying grant programs, USDA may also provide additional application support for these local governments or tribal governments. Support may include offering flexibility in meeting certain application requirements or addressing a barrier to applying for or receiving assistance, such as flexibility related to financial requirements or application scoring criteria.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/2618","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s2618.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:06.697Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-s-2618","plainSummary":"This bill makes it easier for local and tribal governments in rural counties where the federal government owns more than half the land to access certain USDA grant programs. It cuts their required matching funds in half, gives them priority if they haven't received grants in the past decade, and directs USDA to provide extra technical help and flexibility during the application process. The grants cover rural business development, community facilities, telemedicine, and other rural development programs.","industries":[{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"The bill expands access to USDA rural development grants that support farming communities and agribusiness infrastructure in qualifying counties."},{"industry":"telecom","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The bill specifically includes Distance Learning and Telemedicine Grants, which fund broadband and telecommunications infrastructure in rural areas."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Community Facilities grants often fund construction of public buildings and infrastructure, creating demand for contractors in eligible counties."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"5db9519022c8d313804a7e77","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:42:26.041Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would widen access to qualifying Department of Agriculture grant programs for local governments or tribal governments located within a high-density public land county.","whoItTouches":"Farmers, and anyone who buys food.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in farming, and food prices. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-s-2618","bioguideId":"C001113","cycle":2026,"score":0.011170749534038992,"matches":[{"industry":"agriculture","donorAmount":18010,"donorShare":0.01695619040101494,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.006594074044839143},{"industry":"telecom","donorAmount":12500,"donorShare":0.011768594115085327,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.003922864705028443},{"industry":"construction","donorAmount":2500,"donorShare":0.0023537188230170654,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0006538107841714071}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.8257042966929987},"member":{"name":"Catherine Cortez Masto","chamber":"Senate","state":"NV","imageUrl":"data/portraits/C001113.jpg","role":"Sponsor"},"donorProfile":{"bioguideId":"C001113","cycle":2026,"totalItemized":1062148.96,"byIndustry":[{"industry":"finance-banking","amount":44000,"share":0.04142545128510035,"contributionCount":12},{"industry":"insurance","amount":18500,"share":0.017417519290326283,"contributionCount":11},{"industry":"agriculture","amount":18010,"share":0.01695619040101494,"contributionCount":4},{"industry":"pharma","amount":14000,"share":0.013180825408895567,"contributionCount":5},{"industry":"utilities-electric","amount":14000,"share":0.013180825408895567,"contributionCount":5},{"industry":"telecom","amount":12500,"share":0.011768594115085327,"contributionCount":7},{"industry":"health-providers","amount"